You can achieve this by using **Flexbox** on the parent `div`.

Here is the corrected CSS:

```css
div {
    width: 100vh;
    height: 100vh;
    display: flex; /* 1. Enable Flexbox */
    justify-content: center; /* 2. Center horizontally along the main axis (row by default) */
    align-items: center; /* 3. Center vertically along the cross axis */
}
```

### Explanation:

1.  **`display: flex;`**: This turns the parent `div` into a flex container, allowing you to use flex properties on its direct children (the `<img>` tag).
2.  **`justify-content: center;`**: This aligns the children along the **main axis**. By default, the main axis is horizontal (left to right).
3.  **`align-items: center;`**: This aligns the children along the **cross axis**. By default, the cross axis is vertical (top to bottom).

### Complete Working Example (HTML/CSS):

```html
<style>
    /* Apply the centering styles to the parent div */
    div {
        width: 100vh;
        height: 100vh;
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center; /* Horizontal centering */
        align-items: center;    /* Vertical centering */
        border: 1px solid red; /* Optional: to visualize the parent area */
    }

    img {
        /* Optional: to ensure the image doesn't overflow if its natural size is large */
        max-width: 100%; 
        max-height: 100%;
        display: block; /* Good practice for images */
    }
</style>

<div>
    <img src="img/world.png" alt="World Map">
</div>
```

**Note:** If you were strictly restricted to *only* using CSS on the parent, this Flexbox solution is the modern and most reliable way. Older methods (like absolute positioning with `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`) would require setting `position: relative` on the parent, which is still CSS on the parent. However, **Flexbox is cleaner for centering.**

